          "ruleId": "CWE-798",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Hard-coded RSA private key lets anyone mint session JWTs accepted as any user, including admin.\n\nThe RSA private key literal in source is the signing key for every session token (lib/insecurity.ts:54 authorize). Its modulus matches encryptionkeys/jwt.pub (verified by decoding both DER blobs), which updateAuthenticatedUsers (lib/insecurity.ts:189) and isAuthorized (lib/insecurity.ts:52) trust. Attacker-chosen JWT payloads are therefore accepted and stored in authenticatedUsers.tokenMap, so route handlers act on the forged identity. The flow leaves routes/ at lib/insecurity.ts:186-197 and is reported here because it is the root of the session authority that routes/ rely on.\n\nImpact: Full authentication bypass for every account, including admin. Any session-gated data or action (profile, basket, orders, password change, user listing, deluxe, wallet) becomes reachable without credentials.\n\nExploit scenario: An attacker copies the RSA private key from the public repository, signs an RS256 JWT whose payload is {data:{id:1,email:'admin@juice-sh.op',role:'admin'}}, and sends it as Cookie token or Authorization Bearer. updateAuthenticatedUsers verifies it against the public key and caches it. Requests then run as admin, for example GET /rest/user/change-password with no current password (see the changePassword finding) to take over the admin account.\n\nPreconditions: Access to the public source, where the private key is committed.\n\nRecommendation: Treat the current key pair as compromised and rotate it. Remove the private key literal from lib/insecurity.ts. Load the signing key from a deployment secret or a file outside the repository (for example, read PRIVATE_KEY_PEM from env at startup and fail closed if absent). Keep only the public key in the repo. Verify tokens with an explicit algorithm allow-list (algorithms: ['RS256']) on every verify call.\n\nThe source line is not quoted because it holds the credential.\n\nCWE-321. Severity CRITICAL, confidence high."
          },
