          "ruleId": "CWE-94",
          "ruleIndex": 8,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Code injection: stored username passed to eval() on GET /profile.\n\nThe attacker-chosen username stored by updateUserProfile is passed to eval() when the usernameXss challenge is enabled, which is the default outside Docker and Heroku.\n\nImpact: Arbitrary OS command execution as the server process. An attacker can read configuration, keys and the database, modify data, and pivot to the host or network.\n\nExploit scenario: Register an account (POST /api/Users is open), log in, then POST /profile with urlencoded body username=#{process.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync('id')} (use a single line, no double quotes, no ampersands). Issue GET /profile. The eval runs inside the CommonJS module scope, where require is available, so the OS command executes with the Node process's privileges. The username setter only strips HTML tags, so the #{...} payload is stored unchanged.\n\nPreconditions: usernameXssChallenge enabled: not running with Docker or Heroku, and config challenges.safetyMode is auto or enabled (default auto); An authenticated session, obtainable through open self-registration.\n\nRecommendation: Remove the eval path entirely and never execute data derived from username. Patch for routes/userProfile.ts: delete the if/else block at lines 57-77, keep the escaped placeholder unconditionally, and pass data as pug locals:\n\ntemplate = template.replace(/_username_/g, '#{username}') // pug escapes interpolation\n...\nconst fn = pug.compile(template)\n...\nres.send(fn({ username: user.username ?? '', email: user.email, role: user.role, profileImage: user.profileImage }))\n\nThis also removes the template-injection path reported separately at line 73. The intentional SSTI challenge behaviour is lost by design.\n\nCWE-95. Severity HIGH, confidence high."
          },
