          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Session JWT verified without pinning algorithm; HS256 tokens keyed with the public PEM are accepted.\n\nupdateAuthenticatedUsers passes the attacker-supplied token to jwt.verify with the RSA public PEM as the secret and no algorithms option, and package.json pins jsonwebtoken 0.4.0 (pre-4.2.2, which does not restrict algorithms). The project's own jwtForgedChallenge check in routes/verify.ts:91 and :125-128 depends on exactly this HS256-with-public-key acceptance. Data flow leaves routes/ into lib/insecurity.ts.\n\nImpact: Authentication bypass to any account without knowledge of the private key. Remains exploitable after the RSA key is rotated, so it must be fixed separately.\n\nExploit scenario: Attacker downloads encryptionkeys/jwt.pub (served by server.ts:303), builds header alg=HS256 and payload data.role=admin, signs it with HMAC-SHA256 keyed by the PEM text, and presents it. jwt.verify accepts it, the token is cached, and privileged routes accept it.\n\nPreconditions: jsonwebtoken 0.4.0 behaves as described (inferred from version pin and the project's own challenge logic; not executed).\n\nRecommendation: Upgrade jsonwebtoken to a maintained major and pin the algorithm everywhere:\n\njwt.verify(token, publicKey, { algorithms: ['RS256'] }, cb)\n\nIn lib/insecurity.ts verify(), use jws.verify(token, 'RS256', publicKey). In isAuthorized(), use expressJwt({ secret: publicKey, algorithms: ['RS256'] }) on a current express-jwt major. Reject any header whose alg is not RS256 before verification.\n\nCWE-347. Severity HIGH, confidence medium."
          },
