        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-502",
          "ruleIndex": 14,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "YAML deserialization code execution via js-yaml 3.x full-schema load on upload.\n\nAttacker .yml content from an unauthenticated upload is passed to yaml.load (full schema in js-yaml 3.x) and the result is JSON.stringify'd, which invokes any callable own toJSON property, so a !!js/function value runs attacker code.\n\nImpact: Code execution as the Node server user on native (non-Docker) deployments, with the command output returned to the attacker. Full confidentiality and integrity impact on the host.\n\nExploit scenario: POST /file-upload with a file named x.yml whose top-level mapping has a toJSON key whose value is a !!js/function tag containing a function body that runs a system command. JSON.stringify calls it inside the vm, the output comes back in the 410 error text, truncated to 400 characters.\n\nPreconditions: deprecatedInterfaceChallenge enabled: disabledEnv Docker, Heroku, Windows; enabled on a native host in auto safety mode; No authentication required; Reasoned from js-yaml 3.x source behavior; no node_modules in the checkout, so not executed or verified.\n\nRecommendation: Remove the parse. The handler is deprecated, so return 410 without vm or yaml.load, as the else branch at lines 130-133 does. If parsing must stay, use yaml.safeLoad(data) on js-yaml 3.x (or load() on js-yaml 4.x, which is safe by default), drop the vm context, and do not echo the parsed result.\n\nCWE-502. Severity HIGH, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/fileUpload.ts",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
