          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JWT verification does not pin RS256, allowing HS256 forgery using the public key.\n\nverify() passes the PEM public key to jws.verify with no algorithm allow-list, so the token's own alg header selects the verifier. An HS256 token signed with the public key as the HMAC secret is then accepted. The same gap is present in isAuthorized (line 52, express-jwt with secret only, no algorithms option) and updateAuthenticatedUsers (line 189, jwt.verify with no algorithms option). The repo's own challenge at routes/verify.ts:116-133 is solved only when jwt.verify(HS256 token, publicKey) returns no error, which indicates the pinned jsonwebtoken 0.4.0 (package.json:123) accepts this. Routes that depend on verify(): order.ts:88,132 and delivery.ts:20,39 and basketItems.ts:94 (isDeluxe), server.ts:664-665 (isAccounting on order-history), currentUser.ts:17, 2fa.ts:20 and 115. Confidence is MEDIUM because node_modules and any lockfile are absent, so the installed jws and express-jwt could not be read; the conclusion rests on the two-argument call, the pinned versions and the repo's own challenge code.\n\nImpact: Unauthenticated read of all customer orders (emails masked, addressId, paymentId, product lists) and write access to delivery status. Deluxe pricing and purchase-limit bypass. Possibly full impersonation across authenticated routes if express-jwt behaves the same way.\n\nExploit scenario: The attacker downloads /encryptionkeys/jwt.pub, which is publicly served. They craft a JWT with header alg HS256 and payload data.role 'accounting' (or 'deluxe' with a deluxeToken), signed with HMAC-SHA256 keyed by the PEM text. GET /rest/order-history/orders passes isAccounting (server.ts:664) and returns all orders. PUT /rest/order-history/:id/delivery-status (server.ts:665) also passes. If the same confusion applies to isAuthorized (line 52, not verified here), every authenticated route is open to any user id.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker can fetch jwt.pub, which is publicly served; Installed jws/express-jwt accept HMAC verification with a PEM key when alg is HS256 (consistent with the pinned versions and the repo's challenge, not executed).\n\nRecommendation: Pin the algorithm everywhere a token is verified. Replace verify with: export const verify = (token: string) => { if (!token) return false; try { jwt.verify(token, publicKey, { algorithms: ['RS256'] }); return true } catch { return false } }. Change line 52 to expressJwt({ secret: publicKey, algorithms: ['RS256'] } as any) and pass { algorithms: ['RS256'] } at line 189. Upgrade express-jwt and jsonwebtoken (package.json:109, :123) to current majors that reject HMAC verification against a PEM key, and add a test that an HS256 token signed with the public key is refused.\n\nCWE-347. Severity HIGH, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/insecurity.ts",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 55,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "export const verify = (token: string) => token ? (jws.verify as ((token: string, secret: string) => boolean))(token, publicKey) : false"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "verify",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "verify"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F27",
