        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-552",
          "ruleIndex": 15,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Customer order PDFs with email are written into the publicly listed and served /ftp directory.\n\nplaceOrder writes each order PDF, which includes the unmasked customer email (order.ts:65), into ftp/. server.ts:294 serves ftp/ with directory listing and server.ts:295 with a file route that allows .pdf (routes/fileServer.ts:56-58). Any anonymous visitor can list every order file and download it.\n\nImpact: Unauthenticated disclosure of every customer's email address and purchase contents. The data does not include passwords or payment credentials.\n\nExploit scenario: Attacker requests GET /ftp to get the full file listing of order_<hash>-<hex>.pdf names, then downloads each one from /ftp/<name>. Each PDF shows the customer's full email, purchased items, totals and order number.\n\nPreconditions: At least one order has been placed (normal application use).\n\nRecommendation: Write invoices outside any statically served directory (for example data/invoices/, which is not mounted). Serve them through an authenticated route that loads the order and returns it only when order.UserId matches req.body.UserId (set by appendUserId), otherwise 404. Drop the raw email from the PDF. Remove existing PDFs from ftp/ and stop listing that directory. Patch: `const pdfPath = path.join('data/invoices/', pdfFile)` then `fs.createWriteStream(pdfPath)`, and add `router.get('/rest/order/:orderId/invoice', security.isAuthorized(), security.appendUserId(), ...)` with an ownership check.\n\nCWE-552. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
