        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-916",
          "ruleIndex": 18,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Passwords hashed with unsalted MD5 and no key-stretching.\n\nThe shared hash helper is unsalted MD5, and it is used for password storage (models/user.ts:73 setter), password comparison in the login query (routes/login.ts:34), current-password checks (routes/changePassword.ts:39) and 2FA setup/disable (routes/2fa.ts:107, 152). The attacker-supplied password reaches this weak hash on every login. The defining line is in lib/insecurity.ts, reached from routes via these calls. Also CWE-328: The only password hash is MD5 with no salt or work factor, and it is stored by the user model and compared in SQL and in several routes.\n\nImpact: Recovery of plaintext passwords for all accounts from a database or hash dump, and password reuse across other services.\n\nExploit scenario: After any database read or hash exposure (e.g. the password-hash leaks in findings above), an attacker cracks unsalted MD5 at GPU speed, with rainbow tables for common passwords.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker obtains the password hash table or a token that carries it (see the session payload finding).\n\nRecommendation: Use a memory-hard KDF with a per-user salt, for example argon2id via @node-rs/argon2, or scrypt:\n\nimport { hash, verify } from '@node-rs/argon2'\nexport const hashPassword = (pw: string) => hash(pw)\n\nOn successful login with a legacy MD5 hash, verify the MD5 and then store the new hash. Fetch the user row by email and verify with argon2 instead of a string-built query. Also CWE-328: Replace with argon2id, bcrypt, or scrypt with a per-user salt. Rehash on successful login and drop MD5. Keep a separately named non-security hash function for gravatar and ID uses.\n\nCWE-916. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/insecurity.ts",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 41,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "export const hash = (data: string) => crypto.createHash('md5').update(data).digest('hex')"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "hash",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "hash"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F35",
              "title": "Passwords hashed with unsalted MD5 and no key-stretching",
              "impact": "Recovery of plaintext passwords for all accounts from a database or hash dump, and password reuse across other services.",
              "file": "lib/insecurity.ts",
              "line": 41,
              "description": "The shared hash helper is unsalted MD5, and it is used for password storage (models/user.ts:73 setter), password comparison in the login query (routes/login.ts:34), current-password checks (routes/changePassword.ts:39) and 2FA setup/disable (routes/2fa.ts:107, 152). The attacker-supplied password reaches this weak hash on every login. The defining line is in lib/insecurity.ts, reached from routes via these calls. Also CWE-328: The only password hash is MD5 with no salt or work factor, and it is stored by the user model and compared in SQL and in several routes.",
              "exploit_scenario": "After any database read or hash exposure (e.g. the password-hash leaks in findings above), an attacker cracks unsalted MD5 at GPU speed, with rainbow tables for common passwords.",
              "preconditions": [
                "Attacker obtains the password hash table or a token that carries it (see the session payload finding)"
              ],
              "category": "Use of Password Hash With Insufficient Computational Effort",
              "severity": "MEDIUM",
              "confidence": "high",
              "recommendation": "Use a memory-hard KDF with a per-user salt, for example argon2id via @node-rs/argon2, or scrypt:\n\nimport { hash, verify } from '@node-rs/argon2'\nexport const hashPassword = (pw: string) => hash(pw)\n\nOn successful login with a legacy MD5 hash, verify the MD5 and then store the new hash. Fetch the user row by email and verify with argon2 instead of a string-built query. Also CWE-328: Replace with argon2id, bcrypt, or scrypt with a per-user salt. Rehash on successful login and drop MD5. Keep a separately named non-security hash function for gravatar and ID uses.",
              "cwe_id": "CWE-916",
              "snippet": "export const hash = (data: string) => crypto.createHash('md5').update(data).digest('hex')",
              "symbol": "hash",
              "declared_line": 41,
              "via_change": null,
              "other_cwe_ids": [
                "CWE-328"
              ],
              "claudeSecurityPluginFindingId": "v3:bbe2b9790f8a2f2e2dcf5a2baed1997489e2d0411debf791bd0aa6e67f031187",
              "verification": {
