        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-436",
          "ruleIndex": 20,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Duplicate BasketId keys: ownership check and persisted value come from different parses of the same body.\n\nThe untrusted source is the raw request body (req.rawBody, server.ts:349-350), parsed by the streaming parser parseJsonCustom (lib/utils.ts:226-238), which records every key occurrence in order. The ownership check at line 37 compares user.bid to basketIds[0] (first occurrence). The item is saved with basketIds[basketIds.length - 1] (last occurrence) at line 42. The app's own JSON parse (JSON.parse, server.ts:350-360, and req.body used by quantityCheck) takes the last value, so two parsers disagree about which BasketId applies. Also CWE-639: addBasketItem parses the raw JSON body into an ordered key list (utils.parseJsonCustom). It checks basketIds[0] (line 37) and saves basketIds[basketIds.length-1] (line 42). With duplicate BasketId keys, the check sees the attacker's own id and the row goes into the victim's basket. req.rawBody is the untouched string (server.ts:349, express.text at server.ts:352), so the duplicate survives.\n\nImpact: Integrity of another user's basket: an attacker can add items to a victim's basket and cause unwanted purchases. No read access is gained. The authorization decision itself is the same missing-ownership defect the authz lens covers, but the root cause here is the input-parsing divergence.\n\nExploit scenario: POST /api/BasketItems with the raw JSON body {\"ProductId\":1,\"BasketId\":\"<attacker basket>\",\"BasketId\":\"<victim basket>\",\"quantity\":1}. The check passes on the first BasketId, and the item is saved into the victim's basket. The victim's next checkout then charges them for the attacker-chosen product.\n\nPreconditions: Authenticated account (/api/BasketItems is isAuthorized, server.ts:390).; Knowledge of a victim basket id, which is a small sequential integer.\n\nRecommendation: Use one parsed value for both the check and the write. Drop the streaming parser from this handler and use the already-parsed body (last key wins, same as the rest of the app), or reject bodies containing duplicate keys:\n\nexport function addBasketItem () {\n  return async (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {\n    const user = security.authenticatedUsers.from(req)\n    const { ProductId, BasketId, quantity } = req.body\n    if (user && BasketId !== undefined && Number(user.bid) !== Number(BasketId)) {\n      res.status(401).send('{\\'error\\' : \\'Invalid BasketId\\'}')\n      return\n    }\n    const basketItem = { ProductId, BasketId, quantity }\n    // keep the basketManipulateChallenge hook and the save logic\n  }\n}\n\nAlso reject non-object request bodies, so a text body cannot skip the check. Also CWE-639: Validate and persist the same value. Reject any body with more than one BasketId key, then compare the single value to user.bid: `if (basketIds.length > 1) return res.status(400).send('duplicate BasketId'); const bid = Number(basketIds[0]); if (user && bid !== Number(user.bid)) return res.status(401).send(...)`. Persist the validated bid rather than the last parsed value.\n\nCWE-436. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/basketItems.ts",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 42,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "BasketId: basketIds[basketIds.length - 1],"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "addBasketItem",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "addBasketItem"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F39",
