              "impact": "Integrity of another user's basket: an attacker can add items to a victim's basket and cause unwanted purchases. No read access is gained. The authorization decision itself is the same missing-ownership defect the authz lens covers, but the root cause here is the input-parsing divergence.",
              "file": "routes/basketItems.ts",
              "line": 42,
              "description": "The untrusted source is the raw request body (req.rawBody, server.ts:349-350), parsed by the streaming parser parseJsonCustom (lib/utils.ts:226-238), which records every key occurrence in order. The ownership check at line 37 compares user.bid to basketIds[0] (first occurrence). The item is saved with basketIds[basketIds.length - 1] (last occurrence) at line 42. The app's own JSON parse (JSON.parse, server.ts:350-360, and req.body used by quantityCheck) takes the last value, so two parsers disagree about which BasketId applies. Also CWE-639: addBasketItem parses the raw JSON body into an ordered key list (utils.parseJsonCustom). It checks basketIds[0] (line 37) and saves basketIds[basketIds.length-1] (line 42). With duplicate BasketId keys, the check sees the attacker's own id and the row goes into the victim's basket. req.rawBody is the untouched string (server.ts:349, express.text at server.ts:352), so the duplicate survives.",
              "exploit_scenario": "POST /api/BasketItems with the raw JSON body {\"ProductId\":1,\"BasketId\":\"<attacker basket>\",\"BasketId\":\"<victim basket>\",\"quantity\":1}. The check passes on the first BasketId, and the item is saved into the victim's basket. The victim's next checkout then charges them for the attacker-chosen product.",
