        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "ruleIndex": 23,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated recycle lookup returns other users' UserId and AddressId rows.\n\ngetRecycleItem has no authentication, and its route at server.ts:420 is registered without isAuthorized. server.ts:416 says GET is for logged-in users only. The handler passes JSON.parse(req.params.id) straight into findAll and returns the full rows, including UserId and AddressId from models/recycle.ts.\n\nImpact: Unauthenticated enumeration of recycle requests with user and address linkage. Medium because it exposes identifiers and dates, not contact details.\n\nExploit scenario: Anonymous attacker requests GET /api/Recycles/[1,2,3,...,N]. The JSON array becomes an IN query and returns every matching recycle row with UserId and AddressId, enumerating user and address IDs in bulk.\n\nPreconditions: None (unauthenticated network request).\n\nRecommendation: Require authentication and scope the query to the caller. Keep the server.ts:420 guard, add security.isAuthorized() and security.appendUserId() on that route, and filter with `where: { id, UserId: req.body.UserId }`. Also require a scalar integer id: `const id = Number(req.params.id); if (!Number.isInteger(id)) return res.status(400).send('invalid id')`.\n\nCWE-306. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "routes/recycles.ts",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 11,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "export const getRecycleItem = () => (req: Request, res: Response) => {"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
